WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:06.400
Yes! We just talked about Unreal Engine&nbsp;
5.2 a popular, game development platform&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:15.560
approximately a month ago, and now the new 5.3&nbsp;
version is already here. So what’s new here? Can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:22.880
this really be improved so easily? I mean, the&nbsp;
previous version put up a clinic in simulating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.880 --> 00:00:28.680
pretty much everything we could think about.&nbsp;
In their demo, we could drive a virtual car&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:36.640
and even the tire pressure and compression&nbsp;
was simulated. And so much more. And now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:43.040
5.3 gives us everything that a Wise Fellow&nbsp;
Scholar could ask for - better explosions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:47.880
ray traced effects, better cloth&nbsp;
simulations, and more! Yummy!

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:55.480
First, now hold on to your papers Fellow Scholars,&nbsp;
because they now support sparse volume textures.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.480 --> 00:01:04.080
Oh my! Yes! This enables us to create or bring in&nbsp;
already existing volumetric simulations of smoke,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:11.880
explosions, and more. And it also supports&nbsp;
OpenVDB. This is a software library that helps us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:18.640
compress down the immense amount of data that is&nbsp;
required for these simulations. Not so long ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:26.360
we looked at NVIDIA’s amazing NeuralVDB paper,&nbsp;
a variant of this that can cut down the memory&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.360 --> 00:01:36.360
footprint of such a simulation by get this,&nbsp;
up to 100x. 100x less memory needed. Wow.

00:01:36.360 --> 00:01:43.400
And now, we can not only create or import&nbsp;
these simulations, but we can also render&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:48.480
them. Oh yes, that is light transport.&nbsp;
Ray tracing if you will. Something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.480 --> 00:01:54.960
we talk about all the time here, on Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers. Here, we start out with a noisy image,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.960 --> 00:02:01.440
and over time, it clears up, or if we don’t&nbsp;
have the time, we can use a noise filtering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:08.520
algorithm to fill in the missing details. This&nbsp;
is typically for off-line applications here and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:15.000
not for real-time games, but typically for&nbsp;
feature-length movies. But, don’t forget,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:21.800
real time ray tracing is also possible, and it&nbsp;
might come to Unreal Engine quicker than we think.

00:02:21.800 --> 00:02:28.680
They also have a new skeletal editor, where I&nbsp;
really liked that now you can also paint these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:35.080
skinning weights right on the body. Okay,&nbsp;
but what does that mean? Skinning weights&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:42.000
determine the influence of joints and bones&nbsp;
on the body, and how the body should move and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:49.000
deform around them. This can now be done much&nbsp;
more efficiently directly in Unreal Engine.

00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:56.120
Cloth simulations, now are based on the XPBD&nbsp;
paper, extended position-based dynamics. A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.120 --> 00:03:03.880
legendary NVIDIA paper from 2016, it finally&nbsp;
made it to the industry and into the hands of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:10.200
millions and millions of users. I am so happy&nbsp;
to see this kind of tech transfer happen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:16.440
and it really shows the world that these papers&nbsp;
that you hear about here are real. They get out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.440 --> 00:03:23.360
there in the real world and make it better. Note&nbsp;
that they also have an incredible AI-based cloth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.360 --> 00:03:30.680
simulator too that can simulate muscle movements&nbsp;
too. These results are truly something else. Wow.

00:03:30.680 --> 00:03:34.680
They also introduced orthographic&nbsp;
rendering to Unreal Engine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.680 --> 00:03:40.680
this helps us eliminate the distortion that&nbsp;
arises from the shape of real camera lenses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:45.960
leading to a really distinctive look.&nbsp;
This is going to be great for not only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.960 --> 00:03:51.160
architectural visualization projects,&nbsp;
but for top-down strategy games too.

00:03:51.160 --> 00:03:55.040
And there is so much more!&nbsp;
They added camera rig rails,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.040 --> 00:04:00.960
that makes your in-game cameras move a&nbsp;
little more like a real cinematic camera,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.520 --> 00:04:08.160
these amazing virtual LED production stages&nbsp;
can now run with higher resolution and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.160 --> 00:04:13.840
higher frame rates, wide-angle lenses,&nbsp;
multi-camera shoots at the same time.

00:04:13.840 --> 00:04:21.760
Also, something that I don’t hear too many people&nbsp;
talking about is support for foveated rendering.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:27.800
This is for those virtual reality headsets that&nbsp;
can track where you look, and create more detailed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.800 --> 00:04:35.080
geometry around that spot, and do less work on the&nbsp;
parts you are currently not looking at. Something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.080 --> 00:04:42.080
that we talked about almost 400 episodes ago, and&nbsp;
now, once again, it is making its way into the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.080 --> 00:04:49.960
hands of the whole world. Once again, to my joy,&nbsp;
the papers coming alive. What a time to be alive!

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:53.200
Lighting with volumetric fog is also better,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.200 --> 00:04:59.080
these beautiful multi-layer materials&nbsp;
are rendered better. So cool! This is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.080 --> 00:05:04.880
going to be a huge improvement for anyone&nbsp;
creating video games, animation movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:04.880 --> 00:05:11.800
virtual worlds, and many other creative&nbsp;
things. So now, let the experiments begin!

00:06:10.520 --> 00:06:16.400
This was Two Minute Papers with Dr. Károly&nbsp;
Zsolnai-Fehér. Subscribe if you wish to see more.

